{"id":1450,"date":"2020-11-27T19:44:57","date_gmt":"2020-11-28T03:44:57","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1450"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:40:36","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:40:36","slug":"program-the-dc-motors","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/program-the-dc-motors\/","title":{"rendered":"Program the DC Motors"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221607d0b03\" name=\"6a221607d0b03\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Including the library<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Start your code by\u00a0including the Smart Inventor Library. This should be the first thing in your program, even before void setup():<\/p>\n<p><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">1 #include &lt;SmartInventor.h&gt;<\/code><\/p>\n<p><strong>Wait&#8230;what\u2019s a library again?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>You&#8217;ll probably be using libraries in most of your robot programs. Libraries are sets of reusable functions and resources, and can help you with your own code once you use them.<\/p>\n<p>Any code that starts with <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SmartInventor._____<\/code>\u00a0 is a function in the Smart Inventor\u00a0library. \u00a0Any variable in orange is an initialized variable from\u00a0the library that you included.\u00a0If you need to find the libraries that you&#8217;re working with, go to Sketch &gt; Library in Arduino.<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/www.arduino.cc\/en\/Reference\/Libraries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Learn more on the Arduino website.<\/a><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221607d0c4e\" name=\"6a221607d0c4e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Void setup and void loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__step-content\" data-testid=\"activityStep\">\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Inside void setup(), enter the code below so the DC motors are initialized:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SmartInventor.DCMotorUse();<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Inside void loop(), enter the code below to program your DC motor&#8217;s movement:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SmartInventor.DCMotor(M1,CCW,100);<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The DC motor&#8217;s movement has three important parts in its parentheses:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The first two characters are the name of the motor. Entries for the name of the motor are either\u00a0M1, M2, M3, or M4. This refers to which motors you will be controlling. The pins on the Smart Inventor Board are paired, meaning that when the pin for M1 is used, the pin for M3 will also operate. The same thing goes with M2 and M4.<\/li>\n<li>The second part is for the direction your motor will move. There are four entries for directions: CW, CCW, STOP, and LOOSE. CW stands for clockwise, CCW stands for counter clockwise, STOP will stop the motor, and LOOSE will stall the motor to a stop.<\/li>\n<li>The last number is your motor&#8217;s power. Entries for power are positive\u00a0numbers ranging from 0 through 107, with 0 being the lowest and 107 being the highest.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221607d0cb3\" name=\"6a221607d0cb3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Delay<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Finally, include a delay below the code for your DC motor&#8217;s movement. This will give your motor time to change.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">delay(1000);<\/code><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Delays are written with the time in milliseconds in parentheses. You just wrote a delay for 1000 milliseconds, which is 1 second![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221607d0d0c\" name=\"6a221607d0d0c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]This is the final code! However! This will only let you control one motor. If you would like to control and use both motors at the same time, add an extra line of code in void loop(). Use the lesson to help you out!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include&lt;SmartInventor.h&gt; \/\/Includes the Smart Inventor library \r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\nSmartInventor.DCMotorUse(); \/\/Initializes the use of DC Motors\r\nSmartInventor.DCMotor(M1, CCW, 100); \/\/Sets the DC Motor on pin M1 to move counter clockwise direction at power of 10\r\ndelay(1000); \/\/Time step for the previous command\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 5 Including the library [vc_column_text] Start your code by\u00a0including the Smart Inventor Library. This should be the first thing in your program, even before void setup(): 1 #include&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/program-the-dc-motors\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1735,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":true,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":802,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1753,1777,1793,423,853,59,171],"lesson-tag":[2163,2123,2186,2122,2171],"class_list":["post-1450","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-mouse","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-rokit-smart","module-mouse","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1450","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1450\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":8317,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1450\/revisions\/8317"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1735"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1450"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1450"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1450"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}